WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.704
How does spot welding work?

2
00:00:03.254 --> 00:00:05.158
Two steel sheets overlap.

3
00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:11.666
Spot welding joins them at single points, like stitches, with no wire or gas.

4
00:00:12.566 --> 00:00:18.687
A weld gun on a robot squeezes the sheets between two copper electrodes, the tips.

5
00:00:19.237 --> 00:00:24.647
As hard as a weight of 200 to 400 kilos: 2 to 4 kilonewtons.

6
00:00:25.547 --> 00:00:30.249
The transformer on the gun makes a huge current: 8 to 12 kiloamps,

7
00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:32.225
at only a few volts.

8
00:00:33.125 --> 00:00:37.157
The current runs from tip to tip, through both sheets.

9
00:00:37.706 --> 00:00:42.771
Where they touch, the resistance is highest: that is where the steel melts.

10
00:00:43.671 --> 00:00:45.981
Water cools the tips from inside.

11
00:00:46.531 --> 00:00:48.818
In under a second the weld is done.

12
00:00:50.018 --> 00:00:57.010
A body-in-white, the bare car body before painting, has 4,000 to 5,000 spot welds.

13
00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.894
Almost all made by robots.

14
00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:03.139
Spot welding is fast and cheap,

15
00:01:03.339 --> 00:01:07.902
but only for overlapping sheet of 0.6 to 3 millimetres.

16
00:01:08.452 --> 00:01:11.134
From outside you cannot see if a weld is good.

17
00:01:12.334 --> 00:01:13.599
Now slowed down.

18
00:01:14.149 --> 00:01:15.577
The gun squeezes.

19
00:01:16.128 --> 00:01:21.134
Then 9.5 kiloamps flow, for 300 milliseconds.

20
00:01:21.684 --> 00:01:23.611
Then it holds on a moment.

21
00:01:24.511 --> 00:01:28.609
Between the sheets a lens of molten steel grows: the nugget,

22
00:01:28.809 --> 00:01:31.015
4 to 7 millimetres across.

23
00:01:31.565 --> 00:01:33.411
It freezes into the weld.

24
00:01:34.311 --> 00:01:38.583
The current per square millimetre of tip face is the current density.

25
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:46.215
9,500 amps through 28.3 square millimetres gives 336.

26
00:01:47.415 --> 00:01:49.833
Heat grows with the current squared:

27
00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:53.661
10 percent more current gives about 21 percent more heat.

28
00:01:54.561 --> 00:01:57.548
Too much current, or too little force,

29
00:01:57.747 --> 00:02:01.474
and the molten steel sprays out as sparks: expulsion.

30
00:02:02.024 --> 00:02:04.265
That gives dents and a weaker weld.

31
00:02:05.165 --> 00:02:09.893
Too little heat gives a stick weld: it looks fine, but holds nothing.

32
00:02:11.093 --> 00:02:13.868
The tips wear: they get flatter and wider.

33
00:02:14.418 --> 00:02:21.674
After 500 welds the tip face is 7.5 millimetres: 36 percent less current density.

34
00:02:22.224 --> 00:02:23.954
The nugget gets too small.

35
00:02:24.854 --> 00:02:27.756
So the gun regularly goes to the tip dresser.

36
00:02:28.306 --> 00:02:32.033
A spinning blade cuts the tips back to size: tip dressing.

37
00:02:32.933 --> 00:02:37.185
In between, the stepper raises the current, step by step,

38
00:02:37.385 --> 00:02:39.765
so the current density stays the same.

39
00:02:40.665 --> 00:02:41.838
Welds too small?

40
00:02:42.388 --> 00:02:44.617
Do not just turn up the current.

41
00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:47.744
First check that the dresser really cuts.

42
00:02:48.944 --> 00:02:52.264
The weld counter counts the welds since the last dressing.

43
00:02:52.814 --> 00:02:57.551
On galvanised steel that is often 100 to 300 welds.

44
00:02:58.451 --> 00:03:01.602
At 9.0 kiloamps and a 6.0 millimetre tip

45
00:03:01.602 --> 00:03:05.069
face that is 318 amps per square millimetre.

46
00:03:05.619 --> 00:03:09.938
At 6.5 millimetres it takes 10.6 kiloamps.

47
00:03:10.838 --> 00:03:12.800
Each dressing costs copper.

48
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:19.485
When the cap is too short, a cap change follows: old cap off, new one on.

49
00:03:20.385 --> 00:03:25.029
A tip imprint on carbon paper shows whether the tip face is round,

50
00:03:25.229 --> 00:03:27.087
centred and the right size.

51
00:03:27.987 --> 00:03:31.937
If the cooling water drops, the tips get hot and soft.

52
00:03:32.837 --> 00:03:38.331
And the alignment: if the tips are skewed, the nugget turns oval and too small.

53
00:03:39.531 --> 00:03:42.805
In a peel test you pull two welded strips apart.

54
00:03:43.355 --> 00:03:46.977
A good weld tears out as a round plug: the button.

55
00:03:47.877 --> 00:03:50.791
Measure it two ways and take the average.

56
00:03:51.341 --> 00:03:56.533
The requirement is often four times the square root of the thinnest sheet:

57
00:03:56.533 --> 00:03:57.866
at 1.5 millimetres,

58
00:03:58.066 --> 00:03:59.239
4.9.

59
00:03:59.789 --> 00:04:02.378
So an average of 5.4 is good.

60
00:04:03.278 --> 00:04:07.088
If the weld breaks through the weld plane, without a button,

61
00:04:07.289 --> 00:04:09.356
that is interfacial fracture.

62
00:04:09.905 --> 00:04:12.030
In mild steel: a reject.

63
00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:18.053
The chisel test drives a wedge between the sheets, often only to a set gap,

64
00:04:18.253 --> 00:04:20.029
so the part stays usable.

65
00:04:20.929 --> 00:04:26.656
Adaptive control measures every weld as it is made, and corrects current or time.

66
00:04:27.205 --> 00:04:29.388
So you need to break fewer parts.

67
00:04:30.588 --> 00:04:33.421
The weld lobe: current against weld time.

68
00:04:33.971 --> 00:04:37.956
On the left the nugget is too small, on the right it spatters.

69
00:04:38.506 --> 00:04:40.143
The width is your margin.

70
00:04:41.043 --> 00:04:43.156
Each stack-up has its own lobe.

71
00:04:43.706 --> 00:04:48.562
Here 1.2 millimetres on 1.0, at 300 milliseconds.

72
00:04:49.462 --> 00:04:53.992
The top sheet is galvanised DP600, an AHSS.

73
00:04:54.543 --> 00:04:57.399
Lobe: 7.6 to 9.0 kiloamps.

74
00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:03.545
Without zinc 6.9 to 9.1: the zinc makes it 36 percent narrower.

75
00:05:04.445 --> 00:05:09.637
Set the current high, at 8.7: 0.3 below expulsion.

76
00:05:10.187 --> 00:05:12.230
The rest is margin for wear.

77
00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:17.008
AHSS often needs a multi-pulse schedule: a pre-pulse,

78
00:05:17.208 --> 00:05:19.809
a weld pulse and a post-heat pulse.
